\subtree{
\title{0. Decision summary (read this first)}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Gruszka's whole design is "integers in flat arrays, nothing is ever built, everything is hash-consed, memos live inside the value record".} No heap objects per term or value, no reference counting, no GC: a worker frees memory by truncating arrays at the end of an 8 MiB \em{session}. Mathlib: 369 G instructions on 1 worker, 372 G on 4, 2.0-2.4 GB (M2 Pro, PGO); 445 G / 2.47 GB / 25.7 s on the arena's x86 runner including the ndjson->Susz conversion (\code{\verb~|refs/gruszka/README.md:227-248~}).}
\li{\strong{The export is converted once to a binary columnar file and two thirds of it is never materialised globally.} Of mathlib's 105 M expressions, 67 M are reachable from exactly one theorem's proof; they stay as bytes and are decoded into a worker-local scratch arena only when that theorem is checked. Conversion of the 6.18 GB ndjson costs 32-35 G instructions (about 5.5 instructions/byte). Decoding the \em{whole} Susz file through the reader's visitor costs 13.6 G; Gruszka loads only MAIN (38 M expressions) in 1.24 s wall, instruction count not published (\code{\verb~|refs/susz/README.md:69-76,305-313~}, \code{\verb~|refs/gruszka/README.md:153-156~}).}
\li{\strong{The same author's substitution kernel (Pestka) with every trick applied needs 1.07-1.11 T instructions for mathlib; the NbE kernel needs 0.37 T.} Same machine, same input, same "what not to compute" list. That 3x is the measured value of NbE + hash-consed values over locally-nameless substitution.}
\li{\strong{Pestka compiled to wasm-gc on V8 (every term, map and closure a GC object) needs 5.78 T instructions and 11.3 GB for mathlib, 5.2x the native build} (\code{\verb~|refs/pestka/docs/benchmarks.md:99-125~}). This is the best available estimate of what an "objects everywhere" managed-runtime kernel costs on this workload. RLVM must not be that design.}
\li{The "what not to compute" list is cheap to implement and each item is worth 1-10 \% (section 6): unread binder arguments never evaluated, proof arguments not compared, proofs recognised syntactically before inferring, casesOn/matcher go straight to the branch, congruence check after every delta step, failures cached.}
\li{\strong{Cache reuse across declarations is the second big lever}: sessions that only move forward in file order, free variables named by their type so re-opened binders hit the caches, declarations grouped by namespace, contiguous chunks per worker. Pestka measured -9 \% (namespace order), -18..20 \% and a further -4..5 \% (variable naming) on mathlib slices; Gruszka measured -5 \% for contiguous parts vs round robin.}
\li{The elaborate machinery for pathological declarations (regions, ghost keys, ground machine, the race of lazy-delta strategies, deep-work trampoline) exists for CFSG-scale certificates and adversarial tests. On Init/mathlib it is within 0.2-0.7 \% either way. RLVM should implement the reference order plus the cheap shortcuts first, and add budgets later only where the arena's perf tests require them.}
\li{Build-level: PGO trained on Init at 1 worker is worth roughly -11 \% for Gruszka (419.9 G plain at 0.1.2 vs 372 G PGO in the README; the two figures come from different milestones, so approximate) and -12..15 \% for Pestka (stated directly); \code{\verb~|lto = "fat"~}, \code{\verb~|codegen-units = 1~}, \code{\verb~|target-cpu=native~}, mimalloc.}
}

\p{Budget implied for RLVM (x86 arena runner, instructions): 445 G total for the \verb~|#~2 place = about 35 G ndjson->Susz conversion + about 410 G for Gruszka itself (load + check; the split is not published). To beat it RLVM has about 400 G for \em{everything including JIT and GC}.}

